  "query": "SELECT\n  o.id,\n  c.name,\n  o.total\nFROM customers AS c\nJOIN orders AS o\n  ON o.customer_id = c.id\nWHERE\n  c.city = 'Paris'\nORDER BY\n  o.id\nLIMIT 100",
  "diagnosis": "The plan scans the orders table via its primary\u2011key index, then for each row looks up the customer and filters on c.city='Paris'. Because there is no index on customers.city, PostgreSQL cannot restrict the outer scan, so it reads many orders (\u22483000) before the LIMIT. Adding a city index lets the planner start with the small set of Paris customers, and an index on orders.customer_id (or a composite index orders(customer_id,id)) lets the join be performed via index lookups and preserve the ORDER BY o.id without a costly sort.",
